{#- Shared pipeline-step emission, {% import %}-ed by ApiController.java.j2,
    Worker.java.j2, and Job.java.j2 (mirrors every other backend's own
    "one shared macro" choice, made simpler here than Go's own version:
    Java has real exceptions, so a step that fails just throws --
    there is no `if v, err := ...; err != nil` dance to make every
    step's error path explicit, and `result` flows through the
    pipeline as plain `Object` for the identical reason Go's own `any`
    does (a `handler`/`call` step's output has no static relationship
    to its input beyond "whatever the target declares").

    `call` steps render plausible code (mirroring every other target's
    own M3-era templates, which did the same before their own `call`
    infrastructure existed).

    `match` was originally written the same speculative way, casting
    `result` to `java.util.Map<?, ?>` and reading the matched field
    out of it as a generic JSON value -- plausible for a step whose
    predecessor might be untyped, but never actually exercised by any
    25UpdatePlan.md M3 example, and the same speculative shape Go's
    own M7d live proof against `multi-service-media.ciac` caught as a
    real bug: a worker's payload is always deserialized straight into
    its known typed record (`Worker.java.j2`'s own `MAPPER.readValue(raw,
    <PayloadClass>.class)`), so `result` is never a `Map` in the first
    place by the time `match`
    runs whenever `payload_type` is known -- it stays that same
    concrete record type an untyped `handler` step passes straight
    through unchanged (the common case: the seed stub's default `return
    payload;`). Fixed proactively, before any Java example ever hit
    the `ClassCastException`, to cast to `payload_type` and call the
    matched field's own accessor method instead -- the same typed-
    field-access shape Go's own fix (`result.(payload_type).<Field>`)
    and Rust's own `match result.status { ... }` already use. The old
    `Map`-cast branch stays as the fallback for the one context that
    still has no known payload type -- `Job.java.j2` passes
    `payload_type = ""` unconditionally, since a scheduled job has no
    incoming record to type a `match` against -- still inert, no
    example reaches it.

    `handler` steps call a constructor-injected Spring bean field
    (`{{ step.handler.class_name | java_camel }}`) rather than
    constructing one inline the way Go's `services.New{Class}(st)`
    does -- the caller (ApiController/Worker/Job) injects one field
    per distinct handler in `ctx.handlers`/`worker.handlers`/
    `job.handlers`, and Spring resolves each `@Component`-annotated
    `services.<Class>`/`logic.<Class>` bean by type. Every handler
    (classic or typed, M4) exposes the identical `handle` method name
    -- a typed handler's own concrete parameter type means the call
    needs an explicit cast back from the pipeline's `Object result`,
    a classic handler's `Object handle(Object)` needs none. -#}
{% macro emit_step(step, indent, payload_type) %}
{%- if step.kind == "handler" %}
{{ indent }}result = {{ step.handler.class_name | java_camel }}.handle({% if step.handler.is_typed_handler %}({{ payload_type }}) {% endif %}result);
{%- elif step.kind == "publish" %}
{{ indent }}queue.publishJson("{{ step.subject }}", result);
{%- elif step.kind == "call" %}
{{ indent }}result = {{ step.call.class_name | java_camel }}.{{ step.call.method | java_camel }}(result);
{%- elif step.kind == "match" %}
{%- if payload_type %}
{{ indent }}switch (String.valueOf((({{ payload_type }}) result).{{ step.field | java_camel }}())) {
{%- else %}
{{ indent }}switch (String.valueOf(((java.util.Map<?, ?>) result).get("{{ step.field }}"))) {
{%- endif %}
{%- for arm in step.arms %}
{%- if arm.label %}
{{ indent }}    case "{{ arm.label }}" -> {
{%- else %}
{{ indent }}    default -> {
{%- endif %}
{%- for inner in arm.steps %}
{{ emit_step(inner, indent ~ "        ", payload_type) }}
{%- endfor %}
{{ indent }}    }
{%- endfor %}
{{ indent }}}
{%- endif %}
{%- endmacro %}
